            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Great Fire of Rome</strong><br>\nIn 64 AD, a catastrophic fire swept through Rome, destroying large parts of the city. Some ancient sources suggest Nero was responsible for the fire, though this is disputed by modern historians. According to rumors, he was said to have \"fiddled while Rome burned,\" but this saying is figurative, as the fiddle did not exist at that time. Nero did use the fire's destruction as an opportunity to rebuild Rome in a more Hellenistic style and constructed his lavish Domus Aurea, or Golden House.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Persecution of Christians</strong><br>\nNero is often remembered for his persecution of Christians. After the Great Fire of Rome, he reportedly blamed Christians for the disaster, resulting in widespread persecution. Under his orders, many Christians were arrested and executed in horrific manners, which included being fed to wild beasts or burned alive as human torches. This brutal treatment of Christians added to his infamy in historical records.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nero's Artistic Pursuits</strong><br>\nNero had a strong interest in the arts and culture, and he was an avid participant in poetry, music, and theater. He is known to have performed on stage, which was considered inappropriate for someone of his standing. Nero's self-indulgence in the arts was viewed with mixed feelings by the Roman populace and nobility, who often saw an emperor's participation in these events as below the dignity of a Roman leader.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
