            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Revolts</strong><br>\nDuring Nero's reign, several revolts and conspiracies arose. In 65 AD, the Pisonian conspiracy aimed to kill Nero and end his rule, but it failed. Then, in 68 AD, revolts broke out in the provinces of Gaul, Hispania, and Africa, and the rebellion led by the governor of Gaul, Gaius Julius Vindex, gained support. These revolts significantly weakened Nero's position and led to his eventual downfall, showing that despite his initial popularity, dissatisfaction with his rule was growing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Downfall and Death</strong><br>\nNero's rule came to a chaotic end in 68 AD when he was declared an enemy of the state by the Senate. Faced with the revolt led by Vindex and the loss of support from the Praetorian Guard, Nero fled Rome. With few options left and fearing a gruesome execution, Nero chose to take his own life with the assistance of his secretary, Epaphroditos. According to a famous account, his last words were \"What an artist dies in me!\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Nero</strong><br>\nNero's legacy is complex, marred by accounts of tyranny, extravagance, and vice, but also remembered for significant cultural contributions to the Roman Empire. His reign saw the last of the Julio-Claudian dynasty, and after his death, a brief period of civil war ensued, known as the Year of the Four Emperors. Despite the negative aspects of his reign, Nero's impact on culture and the arts continues to be a subject of interest and debate among historians and scholars to this day.</p>\n"
              },
